271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 121 0 R
/T (This property was home to the Lone Star Mine, operating at different periods from the 1910s to 1950s. Mining was for Cinnabar Ore, while the waste material mainly consisted of clay and calcite. The geological formations in this area consist of limestone, which was deposited during the Upper Cretaceous epoch, approximately 66 to 100 million years ago. The Lone Star Mine lies within the Mexican Highland, a region characterized by the geomorphological feature Intermontane Plateaus, which contributes to the unique landscape. Our caves that overlook the domes are on Tres Cuevas Mountain, with an elevation of 3,635. The name means "Three Caves" in Spanish, referring to the three openings on the mountain's northwestern face.)
/E (This property was home to the Lone Star Mine, operating at different periods from the 1910s to 1950s. Mining was for Cinnabar Ore, while the waste material mainly consisted of clay and calcite. The geological formations in this area consist of limestone, which was deposited during the Upper Cretaceous epoch, approximately 66 to 100 million years ago. The Lone Star Mine lies within the Mexican Highland, a region characterized by the geomorphological feature Intermontane Plateaus, which contributes to the unique landscape. Our caves that overlook the domes are on Tres Cuevas Mountain, with an elevation of 3,635. The name means "Three Caves" in Spanish, referring to the three openings on the mountain's northwestern face.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11]
>>
